_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:5:This is the byte/token/surface-language inventory for the independent frontend judgment in Core **Q8.3**, not the ANF syntax of Core. [Q8.3 and Q9.1](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core) distinguish those objects. In particular, Core `copy`, `move`, fresh numeric identities and condition regions are not new source spellings.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:21:| 0 | parameterless `fn main() -> u8`, typed immutable lets, decimal integers, local reads, parentheses, `+`, `return` |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:23:| 2 | all integer widths/bases, integer operators, casts, named wrapping/saturating and lossy conversions |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:47:| 26 | floats and float conversions |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:57:UTF-8 source and UTF-8 static text are supported by [Core Q4.1–Q4.4](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core); the explicit legacy source-encoding and escape rule is [SYN-5](../legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md#SYN-5). ASCII char literals are decided by [OD137](../docs/decisions.md#od137). Exact source whitespace, identifier alphabet and source byte edge cases lack a dedicated decision: **OPEN-1**, **OPEN-2**. Recommended lexical core:
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:99:STRING  = '"', {string_character | string_escape}, '"' ;
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:100:CHAR    = "'", (ascii_char_character | char_escape), "'" ;
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:105:Decimal integers arrive at 0; other bases at 2; FLOAT at 26; STRING/CHAR at 16 (a reason STRING in an earlier annotation is metadata, not a runtime StaticStr feature). Leading-zero decimal integers are explicitly refused: [slice-0 surface](../docs/decisions.md#decided-slice0-surface-2026-11-06). The old forms are documented by [SYN-6](../legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md#SYN-6). **OPEN-5**: approve lowercase base prefixes, no separators, float digit/point syntax and a minimal typed-integer suffix policy and boundary handling; the frozen lexer also accepted uppercase prefixes. The [approved Q6.2 field example](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields) contains `200u8`. A blanket suffix refusal conflicts with that example; OPEN-5 recommends retaining typed integer suffixes, but their complete width/base/boundary grammar needs approval. The displayed INT production is the unsuffixed kernel, not a complete suffix grammar. No exponent notation, `1.`, `.5`, hex floats or special NaN/Inf literals are proposed.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:107:Minus is an operator, not part of INT/FLOAT. Literal expected typing and range checking include negation ([OD31](../docs/decisions.md#od31), [OD63](../docs/decisions.md#od63), [OD138](../docs/decisions.md#od138)); an unconstrained literal is refused, not defaulted. Float literals round directly to their expected width; no lexical suffix is implied by that rule.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:109:Only these escapes are proposed, copied exactly from SYN-5/6; **OPEN-6** requests explicit owner ratification where decisions.md does not enumerate them:
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:111:| Literal | Allowed escapes | Raw content |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:128:`'` and `"` delimit lexical tokens, not standalone expression operators. `..` is only a pattern payload-rest marker (slice 10), not a range expression or update. `->` is only a result arrow (5; main already 0). `=>` is the match-arm arrow (10), fixed by [OD128](../docs/decisions.md#od128). No postfix `?`, `->`, `++`, `--`, compound assignments, range `..=`, or implication operator is introduced. Maximal munch, the precise token inventory and adjacent-operator handling are **OPEN-7**, with [SYN-7](../legacy/docs/spec/syntax.md#SYN-7)'s special split of nested type `>>` retained for approval. A forbidden construct may be recognized to issue a targeted diagnostic without being a valid production.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:147:| 12 | prefix `- ! ~ & &mut *` | right | integer 2, `!` 3, references/deref 13, raw 24 |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:341:First matching arm wins and unreachable arms are refused ([OD18](../docs/decisions.md#od18)); integer matching always needs a catch-all, even after every literal ([OD41](../docs/decisions.md#od41)). Borrowed-scrutinee payload binders are always references ([OD3](../docs/decisions.md#od3)); the source does not invent `ref` patterns. Pattern completeness, duplicate field checking and linear wildcard restrictions are judgments after parsing.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:390:| while/counting loop | 4 | exact `while cond {…}` and `for i from a until b {…}` [OD47](../docs/decisions.md#od47); bounds constrained to one expected integer type [OD48](../docs/decisions.md#od48) |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:394:| assert and theorem suffix | 8 | [OD79](../docs/decisions.md#od79), [SPARK](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-spark-2026-11-06); suffix only on parenthesized assertion per decided example; exact factoring **OPEN-13** |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:400:`deferred_call` is a parsed postfix call whose final suffix is `call_args` (a method call counts); its result must be Unit. It shares the section-5 postfix parser and rejects a binary expression, constructor or block. That syntactic restriction is the OPEN-19 recommendation. `statement_block` means a braced statement-mode block under the OPEN-16 algorithm; `arm_block` uses the surrounding match mode; `arm_expr` means section-5 expr without a block-root alternative. `value_contents` is precisely the sequence/tail selection described in that algorithm, whose EBNF expansion remains OPEN-16. These are explicit parameterized parsing obligations, not unexplained extra terminals.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:418:cast          = unary, {"as", cast_type, {postfix_suffix}} ;
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:423:postfix       = primary, {postfix_suffix} ;
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:424:postfix_suffix = call_args | "[", expr, "]" | ".", IDENT, [method_tail] ;
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:450:`head_expr` is the same precedence hierarchy with a separate **head primary**: at enclosing `(`/`[` depth zero it has no constructor-body suffix. Entering grouping, call parentheses, array brackets or index brackets restores ordinary expr for the contents. Thus `if LIMIT {…}` ends the head, while `if test(P { x: 1 }) {…}` permits a literal inside parentheses. `as` also terminates the source of a range borrow, so that source must use the place grammar below rather than unrestricted expr. [OD16](../docs/decisions.md#od16), [OD34](../docs/decisions.md#od34) fix the head rule, not a casing convention.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:467:| constructors `S { f: e }`, `E::V { f: e }` | enum separator [OD77](../docs/decisions.md#od77), argument-based enum inference, otherwise explicit [OD129](../docs/decisions.md#od129); struct literal shell slice 1; explicit expression fields recommended under OPEN-20; empty struct literal already admitted by OD65 and approved slice 1 |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:479:`contract_expr` uses the expression operator grammar over a restricted, pure, non-consuming specification judgment. It is **not** a second permissive executable language. Ordinary runtime calls, allocation, mutation, linear consumption and capabilities do not become contract operations. Calls are to total `spec fn` and explicitly permitted machine operations. Mathematical integer arithmetic is the default; named machine operations retain machine meaning ([OD12](../docs/decisions.md#od12), [OD145](../docs/decisions.md#od145), [OD115](../docs/decisions.md#od115), [OD116](../docs/decisions.md#od116), [CON-3](../legacy/docs/spec/contracts.md#CON-3)). The exact syntactic restriction and allowed specification atom/type vocabulary is **OPEN-13**; the parser can share syntax, while a separate judgment rejects forbidden forms.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:489:| wrapping arithmetic | 2 | `wrapping_add(a,b)`, `wrapping_sub(a,b)`, `wrapping_mul(a,b)`; [OD10](../docs/decisions.md#od10), [OD145](../docs/decisions.md#od145), exact six operations in [Core Q6.2](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core), [ARITH-4](../legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md#ARITH-4) |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:493:| conversions | integer 2; checked Result 12; floats 26 | `truncate::<T>(x)`, `reinterpret::<T>(x)`, `try_convert::<T>(x)`, `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`; [OD99](../docs/decisions.md#od99), [OD106](../docs/decisions.md#od106), [OD107](../docs/decisions.md#od107); exact floating target-argument convention **OPEN-21** |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:494:| try_convert result | 12 | `Result<T,OutOfRange>`, not Option; [OD106](../docs/decisions.md#od106); [ARITH-10](../legacy/docs/spec/arithmetic.md#ARITH-10) |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:499:| fixed-array replacement | 15 | `swap_at`; [approved slice 15](../ROADMAP.md#vertical-slices); later heap-view replacement follows Core Q3.6 |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:516:Slice 13 introduces ordinary reference borrows; 15 array range views; 16 text ranges; 19 heap-cell opening; 21 heap-array opening/views. The spellings `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view {…} [else {…}]` are decided by [Core Q4.7/Q9.5](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core). There is no `in R` ([OD57](../docs/decisions.md#od57)), and no other heap opening form ([OD36](../docs/decisions.md#od36)). No-range else is not proposed; Q4.7's precise optional-clause combination is **OPEN-19**.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:524:Raw types are `*const T` / `*mut T` (slice 24). Dereference/store use unary `*` and place assignment; `as` is not a blanket pointer/integer conversion mechanism: reference-to-raw conversion is gated by [OD98](../docs/decisions.md#od98). Reference-to-reference casts are refused ([OD103](../docs/decisions.md#od103)); pointer/integer round trips use Q3.9’s `expose` / `from_exposed`, not an invented `as` conversion. Integers carry no pointer provenance; every raw access requires typed validity/provenance/aliasing premises (Core Q3.7–Q3.11). Reference-derived pointers stay confined to the borrow and can never reach a kept parameter; kept retention transfers an owned allocation H by value and returns a linear Kept<H> receipt ([OD64](../docs/decisions.md#od64), Core Q3.12). A write derived from shared &T remains forbidden ([OD112](../docs/decisions.md#od112)).
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:536:| Existential formation/opening/repackaging | 19 | explicit pack, lexical unpack, repackage escapes; Q1.3 | **OPEN-22**: `exists<brand A> T`, `pack::<A>(e)`, `unpack p as <brand A> x { … }`; alternative library-shaped scoped eliminator; binder scope/loop-carried packaging also needs approval |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:556:| cast named type `<` vs less-than | FIRST/FOLLOW | OD91: bare cast target has no type-argument suffix; generic target parenthesized; complete composite type expansion OPEN-14 |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:557:| nested generic `>>` vs shift | lexical/context boundary | split into two `>` only while closing generic lists (SYN-7 evidence); shifts unchanged in expression context; OPEN-7 ratifies exact token rule |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:566:| statement expression vs value-block tail | FIRST/FIRST | common-prefix parse with `;`, `=` or `}` suffix; OPEN-16 must supply the fully expanded grammar |
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:631:5. **OPEN-5 — Numeric token details (2/26; leading-zero decision already binds 0).** Recommend lowercase base prefixes, no separators; retain typed integer suffixes to preserve the approved Q6.2 example, with exact suffix grammar still for approval, digits-dot-digits floats without exponents; reject malformed adjacent numeric text as one diagnostic. Alternative: retain uppercase prefixes and add explicit separators/exponent forms. Reason: do not silently contradict `200u8` in the approved field example; freeze the accepted token language independently of lexer accidents; do not reopen decided decimal-leading-zero refusal.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:632:6. **OPEN-6 — Quoted tokens/escapes (16; metadata strings earlier).** Recommend precisely the two escape lists in section 2, no raw line breaks/control characters, char-first closed-quote recognition before labels, malformed quotes refused. Alternative: add hex/Unicode escapes and multiline text. Reason: SYN-5/6 give a small historical set, but source lexical edge cases need explicit approval; Core Q4.2's embedded NUL remains allowed through \0.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:633:7. **OPEN-7 — Punctuation and tokenization (by operator slice).** Recommend exactly section 2's token set, longest match, contextual >> splitting only in generic closers. Alternative: lexer always emits single > tokens and parser composes shifts. Reason: preserve expression shifts and nested generics without whitespace-dependent syntax.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:643:17. **OPEN-17 — Exact kept pattern/arm surface (9/10/16).** Recommend qualified enum patterns, `_` catch-all, integer/bool/char literals, OR patterns, named field renaming, whole-payload `{..}` and a mandatory comma after every arm. Keep irrefutable enum lets, mut applying to every binder, explicit field handling and the deferred status of nested patterns. Alternative: comma only after non-block arms, subject to FIRST/FOLLOW proof. Clarify OD25’s “bare” fieldless wording against the chapter’s qualification rule; explicitly disposition generic `E::<T>::V` and module-qualified let paths. Do not treat the frozen parser as authority for a new accepted form.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:647:21. **OPEN-21 — API/view spellings and branded signatures (7/15/16/19/21–27).** Recommend View/ViewMut second-class parameter constructors and ordinary calls for the decided ct_select/ct_eq/text/container APIs; use exact explicit branded type arguments; floating conversions use `name::<Target>(x)`. Alternative: &[]-style view types and qualified associated APIs. Reason: feature names in Q3/Q4 and the information-flow decision do not decide every arity, type position, qualifier or conversion target syntax. Submit the exhaustive concrete signature table before encoding it; no fabricated complete signatures here.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:648:22. **OPEN-22 — Brand/package/function-contract surface (19/20).** Recommend the explicit brand/exists/pack/unpack proposals in section 7 and named binder contracts on fn types, reusing ordinary explicit context parameters for function values. Alternative: scoped library eliminators plus named contract aliases. Reason: Core Q1 approves these mechanisms, while R-0601 explicitly still owns a binding design; surface syntax cannot be recovered from ANF constructors. Approve each binder, packaging expression, escape and loop-carried existential rule separately.
_review/GRAMMAR_REVIEW.md:663:| 5 | Numeric tokens | Agree no suffixes | No suffixes; revise Q6.2 example | Agree no suffixes | Change: retain suffix evidence from 200u8; ask exact suffix grammar. Do not rewrite an approved example by recommendation. |
