docs/decisions.md:1297:| OD7 | <a id="od7"></a>An unsafe-module marker in source (for example `trusted mod`), which never confers trust by itself; a gate lists the marked modules | D10; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509's trusted-base inventory; RocksDB slice 1 (where its `extern` declarations live); V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: an unsafe-module marker in source (for example `trusted mod`) never confers trust by itself, a gate lists the marked modules, and trust stays per method (OD22) **Revised 2026-11-03: this historical module-marker requirement was superseded by OD61 (2026-11-01), which removed `trusted mod`; trust and inventory remain per declaration.** |
docs/decisions.md:1300:| OD10 | <a id="od10"></a>Arithmetic: checked by default, named wrapping and saturating operations, shifts with a checked count; an abort runs no deferred call | T-ARITH, recorded as settled (today's behaviour), with §4.5's abort rule | exits 2h and 2j, so V1a, V1b and V1c (checked `+ - *` and their traps); R-0493 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: integer arithmetic is checked by default; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations; the shift count is checked; an abort runs no deferred call **Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): plain arithmetic is admitted only with evidence that it cannot fail; wrapping, saturating and checked (`Result`) forms cover the rest; overflow is still never silent.** |
docs/decisions.md:1303:| OD13 | <a id="od13"></a>Raw-pointer ownership: `ptr::read_owned`, an explicit replace or take through `&mut T`, a trusted ownership obligation for a raw address | T-RAWDUP and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (TRUST-7). **Provisional (bug 198):** the current compiler refuses a by-value `*p` of a non-Copy pointee through a raw pointer (E0311) and moves the value out only with the intrinsic `read_owned(p)`, with 26 std sites migrated; provisional until the owner answers | RocksDB slice 1 (its trusted binding code); R-0509, R-0514; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: a value moves out of raw memory only through `read_owned(p)` or an explicit replace or take through `&mut T`, and a raw address carries a trusted ownership obligation; bug 198's provisional repair is final |
docs/decisions.md:1311:| OD21 | <a id="od21"></a>Representation rights of a parent over its child module (added 2026-10-25, from bug 262): when a parent module imports its child's newtype or struct, every stage lets the parent construct the newtype and read and write the struct's private fields, because Check counts the children's declarations among the module's own for construction and field rights; Resolve also lets a parent name a child's newtype as a symbol without an import. Two options: enforce TYPE-11 as written (a newtype or private field is constructed or accessed only inside the module that declares it, so a child is another module), or amend TYPE-11 to grant a parent these rights over the declarations it imports from its children | the behaviour dates from construction rights slice 1 (2026-07-18); it contradicts TYPE-11@1's wording ("outside the module that declares it"); bug 262's record names `tests/programs/module_qualified_impl` as a program that applying TYPE-11 strictly would refuse; the wider impact is not yet measured (no `scripts/tools/rule_impact.sh` scan is recorded); bug 262's record (branch `wip/newtype-ctor-stage`) leaves the question to R-0011 and makes no recommendation | R-0011; the construction-rights fixtures (`module_qualified_impl` and bug 262's `error_262_*` and `regress_262_*` fixtures); bug 262's follow-up (whether Resolve binds a child's newtype in its parent) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: only the declaring module; a newtype is constructed, and a private field read or written, only inside the module that declares it, and a parent module gets no rights over its child (TYPE-11 enforced as written) |
docs/decisions.md:1314:| OD24 | <a id="od24"></a>STMT-7: `main` returns `u8` or `Unit` (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | the current compiler accepts any result type, masks an integer to 0–255 and maps `bool` natively; bug 320 | exit 2q; V1c | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): `main` returns `u8` or `Unit` (exit 0); no masking, no bool mapping, no other result type. Now STMT-7@3 (partial, bug 320). Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner: `u8` only ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-main)) |
docs/decisions.md:1316:| OD26 | <a id="od26"></a>TYPE-6 with TRUST-1: operators take numbers only (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | trusted and `Unsafe` code may compare raw pointers and add an integer to one; bug 322 | exit 2q; R-0502 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): arithmetic, shifts and comparisons take numbers only; pointer offset, difference and ordering are `ptr::offset` and named functions. Now TYPE-6@2 (partial, bug 322) |
docs/decisions.md:1319:| OD29 | <a id="od29"></a>OWN-7: a statement expression has type `Unit` (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | only a syntactically pure non-Unit Copy value is refused (E0294); a call's result is dropped silently; bug 325 | exit 2q; exit 2j | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): a statement expression must have type `Unit`; every other unused result needs `discard(e)`; the syntactic purity heuristic is removed. Now OWN-7@3 (partial, bug 325) |
docs/decisions.md:1321:| OD31 | <a id="od31"></a>ARITH-2: no `Int` fallback for an unconstrained literal (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | an unconstrained integer literal is `Int` and a float literal `Float64`; bug 327 | exit 2q; exit 2j | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): an integer literal takes its type from its context, as today; the fallback to `Int` where nothing is expected is removed, so an unconstrained literal is refused; suffixes are not required; float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Now ARITH-2@2 (partial, bug 327) |
docs/decisions.md:1327:| OD37 | <a id="od37"></a>The names of the pointer difference and ordering functions (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26). Options: `ptr::diff(p, q) -> Int` and `ptr::cmp(p, q) -> Ordering` beside `ptr::offset`; or no ordering at all, only `ptr::addr(p)` (via `ptr::expose`) compared as integers. Recommendation: `ptr::diff` and `ptr::cmp`, both trusted, each with its provenance premise (same allocation) stated as an obligation; Austral has no named pointer-difference or ordering functions, and its `Pointer` and `Address` types are compared with the ordering operators Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): one trusted `ptr::diff` (same-allocation premise), ordering read from its sign, no `ptr::cmp`; specify the representability of its result, one-past pointers included (Q8 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it) | trusted code compares raw pointers with `==` and `<` today (bug 322) | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6, TRUST-1) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One trusted `ptr::diff`, ordering from its sign, no `ptr::cmp` |
docs/decisions.md:1331:| OD41 | <a id="od41"></a>PAT-9 and integer ranges that span their type (added 2026-10-29, while fixing bug 317). Options: a catch-all after ranges covering the whole integer type is not refused (PAT-2 still requires it); or it is refused and PAT-2 is relaxed to accept ranges that cover the type. Recommendation: not refused, keeping PAT-2 as decided Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): close as range-specific under OD56, recording in PAT-2 and PAT-9 that an integer match still needs a catch-all and that listing every literal is not coverage | such a match is accepted | bug 317's repair (PAT-9, PAT-2) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Close as range-specific; integer matches keep a required catch-all, listing every literal is not coverage |
docs/decisions.md:1336:| OD46 | <a id="od46"></a>Generic type-argument inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 308's ruling). Main today binds each type parameter from the types its peek (`PeekType`) computes for the arguments given for it, a value `if` or `match` included since bug 308, but answers `.placeholder` for a generic call's result and some arms with binders, and refuses a parameter no argument determines; it never infers from the expected result type. Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) also infer from the expected type at the call site. Recommendation: (b), local and predictable, with explicit type arguments for the rest; Austral infers type parameters from the arguments, and a `let` declaration's type can disambiguate a return-type-polymorphic call Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) from checked argument types only, never from the expected result; arguments that need an expected type are refused, not defaulted | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TYPE-13, TYPE-15; bug 308's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): From checked argument types only |
docs/decisions.md:1337:| OD47 | <a id="od47"></a>Loops: `while` and a counted range loop (added 2026-10-29) | the current compiler has `while` and the C-style `for (init; cond; step)`, `for (cond)` and `for { }`; bug 331 | exit 2q; R-0499; V1a's loops and exits | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) the C-style `for` is removed in every form; (2) loops are `while cond { ... }` and `for i from a until b { ... }`: half-open (`a`, ..., `b - 1`, empty when `a >= b`), the index immutable in the body, `a` and `b` evaluated once before the first iteration, the index of the bounds' integer type (both bounds of one type), `break` and `continue` as usual (`continue` advances to the next index), `from` and `until` reserved (whether reserved or contextual was reopened by the reviews of that day: OD50), LL(1) as `for` IDENT `from` expr `until` expr block with the restricted head expression for `b` (OD16); (3) in Core the counted loop desugars to the one `while` form, carrying its termination measure `b - i`. Now STMT-6@3, STMT-11@2 and STMT-12 (pending, bug 331) |
docs/decisions.md:1339:| OD49 | <a id="od49"></a>The arithmetic of a counted loop's termination measure (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD47). Options: (a) the measure is a mathematical integer in the proof only, never computed at run time; (b) it is computed in the unsigned type of the bounds' width; (c) a counted loop whose `b - a` cannot be represented is refused. Recommendation: (a): the loop compares `i < b` and increments `i`, which cannot overflow below `b`, so the measure need not exist at run time Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) a mathematical integer in the proof only, never computed at run time | the counted loop is not in the current compiler | bug 331's repair; the contracts chapter's loop variant | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Mathematical integer in the proof only |
docs/decisions.md:1344:| OD54 | <a id="od54"></a>`newtype` and `.0` (added 2026-10-30, item S3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `newtype` declarations and the `.0` projection stay, or a one-field struct with a private field replaces them. Options: (a) keep `newtype` and `.0`; (b) remove both, using a one-field struct whose field is private to its module. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: no newtype; a type is made opaque by the module interface hiding its definition (a record being one possible definition) | `newtype` declarations and `.0` are accepted; `unwrap(id)` is an intrinsic | TYPE-7, TYPE-11, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
docs/decisions.md:1346:| OD56 | <a id="od56"></a>Range patterns (added 2026-10-30, item S11 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether integer range patterns (`1..=9 =>`) stay in the language (this amends OD19). Options: (a) keep them (PAT-7); (b) remove them, the comparison written in the arm or an `if` chain. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove; then close OPEN-41 as range-specific, recording in PAT-2 and PAT-9 that an integer match still needs a catch-all and that listing every literal is not coverage, so that catch-all is never unreachable. Austral: no range patterns | range patterns are accepted (PAT-7) | PAT-7, PAT-2, PAT-9, OPEN-41, OD19 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove; close OD41 as range-specific |
docs/decisions.md:1353:| OD63 | <a id="od63"></a>Float literals take their expected type (added 2026-10-30, item Q4 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a float literal takes the float type its position expects and is refused where nothing is expected, as ARITH-2@2 states under OD31's note that float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Options: (a) yes, as ARITH-2@2 states for integers and floats alike; (b) a float literal defaults to `f64`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a). Austral: not checked | `let x = 1.5;` is accepted as `Float64` (ARITH-2@2 is partial, bug 327) | ARITH-2, ARITH-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Expected type, refused where none |
docs/decisions.md:1354:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
docs/decisions.md:1356:| OD66 | <a id="od66"></a>The unit literal `()` (added 2026-10-30, item review new 2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `()` is the unit value's literal, given that the grammar has no production for it. Options: (a) add `()` to the grammar; (b) no unit literal, Unit produced only by a statement or a Unit call. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: the unit value is written `nil` | the grammar has no production for `()` | SYN-1, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Add `()` |
docs/decisions.md:1381:| OD91 | <a id="od91"></a>A cast followed by `<` (added 2026-10-30, item LL(1) type\|5.2 of grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv). Question: How `x as T < y` parses, given that `<` after a cast's type name may start type arguments or be the less-than operator. Options: (a) a cast's type takes no type arguments unless parenthesized (`x as (T<U>)`), so `<` is less-than; (b) the parser's reading: `<` always starts type arguments, so the comparison needs parentheses (`(x as T) < y`); (c) refuse `<` directly after a cast's type and require parentheses either way. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (in the LL(1) ledger, not the picks). Austral: a conversion is the cast expression `e : T`, which takes no type arguments after it, so the conflict does not arise | the parser always reads type arguments, so `x as T < y` is refused (grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv, row type\|5.2) | SYN-1, SYN-2, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `x as T < y` is less-than; a generic cast type needs parentheses `x as (Vec<T>)` |
docs/decisions.md:1383:| OD93 | <a id="od93"></a>Widening a function value's capability set (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a function value converts to a function type whose capability set contains its own, implicitly by subset or by an explicit conversion. Bears on TYPE-4, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: refused (E0220): `fn(Int) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int`, nor `with(File)` a `with(Std)`, and there is no explicit conversion; a capability-polymorphic parameter accepts either by instantiating its variable. The callable-values design (§10) says there is no implicit widening, and that "widening is explicit or via lattice subsumption only", with a lattice example (`Concurrent ⊇ Async`) whose names are not capabilities; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES R7 keeps handle types invariant, as TYPE-4 does. Options: (a) exact types, as in the current compiler; (b) subsumption into a larger set; (c) an explicit conversion. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-93 | see OPEN-93 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Exact types **Canonical current capability-set rule: authority rule 4; this earlier exact-types answer refers to it.** |
docs/decisions.md:1387:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): every build refuses an operation without discharged evidence.** |
docs/decisions.md:1388:| OD98 | <a id="od98"></a>Pointer and reference conversions written with `as` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Which conversions between raw pointers and references `as` writes (`&x as *const T`, `p as &T`, `p as &mut T`, `p as *mut U`). Bears on TRUST-1, TRUST-3, REF-5, ARITH-5. The current compiler: `&x as *const T` is accepted in safe code, `p as &T` from a raw pointer is accepted in trusted or `Unsafe` code (the binary and `--interp` agree), and a reference cast between different pointee types (`a as &Int` with `a: &A`) is refused (E0553). No entry states these: TRUST-3 forms a reference from a raw pointer with `&*p`, and REF-5 lists borrowing and projection as the ways a reference is formed. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8 kept `as` "the spelling of pointer and reference casts (`as *const T`, `as *mut T`, `as &K`, `as &mut V`)", 281 lines of std. Options: (a) `as` keeps every one of them; (b) `as` converts only a reference to a raw pointer, and a reference is formed from a raw pointer only by `&*p` (TRUST-3); (c) pointer conversions become named functions. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-98 | see OPEN-98 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reference to raw pointer conversion only in trusted/Unsafe code (equal to Austral) |
docs/decisions.md:1389:| OD99 | <a id="od99"></a>The names of the named conversions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: The spellings of the named conversions of ARITH-5, ARITH-10 and ARITH-11 (`try_convert`, `truncate`, `reinterpret`, the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions, and the float roundings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`). Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10, ARITH-11. The current compiler: none of them exists (E0101). ARITH-5's sentence names `truncate` and `reinterpret`, while the archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 calls its spellings indicative, to be fixed by the API's owner; it names the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions by their rounding only. Options: (a) the spellings above, as builtins; (b) other names, or std functions rather than builtins. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-99 | see OPEN-99 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): These spellings, as builtins |
docs/decisions.md:1391:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
docs/decisions.md:1393:| OD103 | <a id="od103"></a>`as` between reference types (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing MEMORY_SEMANTICS). Question: Whether `as` may convert a reference type to a reference type, given that REF-5 lists borrowing and projecting as the only operations that form a reference. Bears on REF-5, REF-6, ARITH-5, TRUST-4. The current compiler: an identity cast between equal reference types is accepted and denotes the operand's place (`read(r as &Int)` with `r: &Int`, and `read(&v as &Int)`, exit 42 on both consumers); every other cast between reference types, `&mut Int as &Int` included, is E0553 at core-check under ARITH-5's tag, whose sentence covers only numeric, `bool` and `char` conversions; a cast from a reference to a raw pointer is allowed (TRUST-4). The design record (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.3) listed a cast of a traced reference binding (`r as &U`) among the reference-forming operations. Options: (a) refuse `as` between reference types; (b) allow the identity cast as a no-op on the operand's place. Conversions between a reference and a raw pointer are OPEN-98's, not this question's. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the MEMORY_SEMANTICS absorption, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-103 | see OPEN-103 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
docs/decisions.md:1396:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
docs/decisions.md:1397:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
docs/decisions.md:1398:| OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
docs/decisions.md:1400:| OD110 | <a id="od110"></a>Enums at the foreign boundary (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value of enum type may cross an `extern fn` boundary, and if so in what representation. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-7. The current compiler: refused: `extern fn f(x: E)` with `enum Copy E { A, B }` is E0574 (a non-FFI-safe parameter); only scalars, raw pointers and `repr(C)` structs cross. The design record [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) says enums must not cross (§3.2); before it was archived it also said they cross as integers. Options: (a) refused, as in the current compiler; (b) a fieldless enum crosses as an integer of a stated width, which needs a stated discriminant for each variant. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | refused (E0574) | FFI-3, FFI-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refused |
docs/decisions.md:1404:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
docs/decisions.md:1408:| OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): refusal of proven violations becomes refusal of unproven safety, everywhere.** |
docs/decisions.md:1412:| OD122 | <a id="od122"></a>A `repr(C)` struct returned from an extern function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[repr(C)]` struct returned from an `extern fn` is returned as the platform's C calling convention returns it, at every size. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-11. The current compiler: a returned struct of at most 8 bytes comes back as one 64-bit integer, stored as 8 bytes into the struct's slot even when the struct is smaller (a 4-byte `in_addr` returned by `inet_makeaddr` reads back wrong natively); a larger one is returned as an LLVM aggregate, which matches C on x86-64 and AArch64 only for some layouts. [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) promised returns of at most 8 bytes and listed larger ones as not promised. Options: (a) every size, as C, classified by the `Layout` authority (R-0502); (b) at most 8 bytes (or 16), a larger return refused in an `extern fn` signature; (c) none, a returned struct going through an out-pointer the binding declares. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-122 | see OPEN-122 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Every size per C ABI via `Layout` |
docs/decisions.md:1413:| OD123 | <a id="od123"></a>`as` on a newtype outside its module (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether `v as N` and `n as T`, between a newtype `N` and its inner type `T`, are accepted outside the module that declares `N`, where TYPE-11 refuses `N(v)`. Bears on TYPE-11, TYPE-24, ARITH-5, OPEN-54. The current compiler: both accepted in any module, so `x as Id` builds a foreign newtype that `Id(x)` may not (E0296), and a validated wrapper's smart constructor can be bypassed. [VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md) §8 describes the cast rule as guarding the wrapper contract from an `as` bypass, which holds for casts to unrelated types only. Options: (a) accepted anywhere, as in the current compiler; (b) accepted only where `N(v)` and `.0` are (TYPE-11, with R-0500's follow-on for `.0`); (c) `as` never names a newtype, the conversions being `N(v)` and `.0`. Austral: no newtype (OPEN-54's comparison). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-123 | see OPEN-123 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `as` never names a newtype |
docs/decisions.md:1414:| OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../legacy/docs/platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
docs/decisions.md:1416:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles. Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): profiles are read-only and separate address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile and its `i32` enum tags are unchanged |
docs/decisions.md:1428:| OD138 | <a id="od138"></a>Which positions give a literal its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which positions give an integer literal operand its expected type under ARITH-2@3 (and a float literal's, should OPEN-63 give floats the same rule). Bears on ARITH-2, TYPE-4 and TYPE-6. The current compiler: an unconstrained integer literal is typed `Int` and a float literal `Float64` (bug 327); with `x: u8`, a literal beside `x` takes `u8` as the other operand of `+`, `<` and `&` and as the count of `>>`: `x + 300`, `x < 300`, `x & 300` and `x >> 300` are each refused natively with E0227 (`integer literal 300 is out of range for type 'u8'`), and `300 as Int` is accepted, whether through `as` or the `Int` default the probe does not show. ARITH-2@3 refuses an integer literal with no expected type but does not list the positions that supply one. Options: (a) declared positions (an annotated binding, a parameter, a return, a field) and the other operand of a binary operator, as the probe shows the current compiler doing for `+`, `<`, `&` and a shift count (other operators were not probed); (b) declared positions only, so a literal beside a typed operand needs a typed binding; (c) option (a), plus the target type of an `as` for its literal operand. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-138 | see OPEN-138 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Declared positions + other operand of a binary op |
docs/decisions.md:1430:| OD140 | <a id="od140"></a>Implicitly dropped results of `free` and of a deferred call (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether the result of `free(h)` (the pointee, of type `T`) when `T` is Copy, and a deferred call's Copy result, may be dropped without `discard`, as exceptions to OWN-7@3 (OD29). Bears on OWN-7, OWN-17, MEM-5 and OWN-15. The current compiler: both accepted: `free(h);` on a `Heap<Int>` (whose result is the `Int` moved out of the cell: `let v: Int = free(h);` after `alloc(42)` gives 42 on both consumers) and `defer f();` with `fn f() -> u8` compile and run, while OWN-7@3 (decided OD29) drops a non-Unit result only through `discard(e)`. OWN-17 states the current compiler's drop of a deferred call's result, and MEM-5 and OWN-15 describe `free(h);` as a statement; neither is named as an exception in OWN-7. Options: (a) both are exceptions, stated in OWN-7; (b) neither is, so each needs `discard`; (c) `free` is an exception and a deferred call's result is not. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-140 | see OPEN-140 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Neither; each needs `discard`. Earlier clarification of 2026-11-02: `free` no longer exists (OD59); a deferred call's Copy result is dropped with `discard`, while a `Result` or `Option` cannot be discarded (OD62) and is handled by a named function that returns `Unit` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)). Revised 2026-11-02: `defer` takes a call whose result is `Unit` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current deferred-result rule: the Unit-only audit rule below; the earlier discard-inside-defer fragment is superseded, and authority rule 9 refers to the Unit-only rule.** |
docs/decisions.md:1435:| OD145 | <a id="od145"></a>How a contract names machine arithmetic (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: How a contract expression names machine arithmetic, which CON-3 allows in place of its default mathematical arithmetic. Bears on CON-3 and ARITH-4. The current compiler: `#[ensures(result == wrapping_add(x, 1))]` is accepted and the program runs (contracts are erased at run time); what the proof pipeline makes of the named operation was not probed. CON-3 says a contract's integer arithmetic is mathematical unless it names a machine operation, and no rule or example names one. Options: (a) the named operations of ARITH-4 (`wrapping_add`, `saturating_add` and the rest) are the machine operations in a contract; (b) a contract-only spelling; (c) contracts have only mathematical arithmetic, and CON-3 drops the exception. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-145 | see OPEN-145 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): ARITH-4 named operations |
docs/decisions.md:1438:| OD148 | <a id="od148"></a>Type parameters on a trait (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether a trait declaration may have type parameters, and if so what binds them at a bound and an impl. Bears on TRAIT-1, TRAIT-9 and SYN-1. The current compiler: `trait Tr<T> { fn get(&self, x: T) -> T; }` parses, but no bound or impl head can supply `T`, so `impl Tr for S { fn get(&self, x: u8) -> u8 ... }` is refused with E0582 and E0587 (`expected return type T, got u8`). OPEN-77 lists generic trait methods among the grammar's stale items; it covers only the productions, while this entry covers what the parameters mean and what binds them. Options: (a) refuse type parameters on a trait declaration; (b) allow them, with type arguments at bounds and impl heads (`impl Tr<u8> for S`). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-148 | see OPEN-148 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse type parameters on a trait |
docs/decisions.md:1440:| OD150 | <a id="od150"></a>Which text governs the grammar until it is resynchronized (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether, until OPEN-77's resynchronization, `grammar/concrete.ebnf` or the rule entries govern where they disagree. Bears on SYN-1 and OPEN-77. The current compiler: the parser accepts forms the EBNF does not derive (`import a.{f};`, `m::f()`, `f::<T>(x)`, value `if` and `match`, `pub` fields, bodyless declarations) and the EBNF keeps forms the entries removed (`E#V`, the C-style `for`, `break <expr>`); SYN-1 calls the EBNF the grammar, and the LL(1) checkers read it. Options: (a) the rule entries govern, and the EBNF is descriptive until the resync; (b) the EBNF governs, and an entry that disagrees is pending; (c) neither: each disagreement is decided one by one under OPEN-77. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-150 | see OPEN-150 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Rule entries govern |
docs/decisions.md:1444:| OD154 | <a id="od154"></a>Whether the string builtins require `Alloc` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether `string_concat`, `string_slice`, `string_substr`, `string_trim`, `int_to_string`, `bool_to_string`, `float_to_string`, `string_push_char`, `string_append`, `string_append_int` and `string_append_bool` require the `Alloc` capability. Bears on MEM-7, TRUST-4 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: Check gives none of these builtins a capability, and a function with no capabilities calls `string_concat` and `string_append_int` and exits 42 on both consumers (the other names were not probed). MEM-7@1 lists `string_reserve` and `append` among the builtins that require `Alloc`; `append` is accepted without `Alloc` (bug 349), and it expands into `string_append`, `string_append_int`, `string_append_bool` and `string_push_char`. MEM-7's rationale says allocation is an effect a signature shows. Options: (a) each builtin named above requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists it; (b) a stated subset of them requires `Alloc`, and MEM-7 lists that subset; (c) only the builtins MEM-7 already lists require `Alloc`, so `string_reserve` and `append` do and the names above do not, and MEM-7 says so; (d) no string builtin requires `Alloc`, `string_reserve` and `append` included, and MEM-7 drops those two. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-154 | see OPEN-154 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Allocating string builtins need `Alloc` (MEM-7; closes bug 349) |
docs/decisions.md:1447:| OD157 | <a id="od157"></a>How the type `Unit` is written (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether the type `Unit`, which STMT-7 names as a result type of `main` and which other rules and the builtins catalog also name, has a spelling in type positions, and which spelling. Bears on STMT-7, TYPE-4, SYN-1 and OPEN-66. The current compiler: a function with no result type returns no value, and `fn main() { return; }` exits 0; `fn main() -> Unit` and `let x: Unit = f();` are refused at resolve (E0108, unknown type 'Unit'); `fn main() -> ()` is a parse error (E0001, `expected type`). STMT-7@3 says `main`'s result type is `u8` or `Unit`. OPEN-66 concerns the unit value's literal; this entry concerns the type in type positions, and the two cross-reference each other. Options: (a) no spelling: the type is written only by omitting the result type, and STMT-7 says so; (b) `Unit` is a builtin type name, accepted wherever a type is; (c) `()` is the type's spelling; (d) both `Unit` and `()` are accepted. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-157 | see OPEN-157 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): The type is written `Unit`, its value `()` |
