1301:| OD11 | <a id="od11"></a>Casts: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function | T-CAST: ARITHMETIC_POLICY §8 (2026-10-21) and §4.5's value-domain rule; not implemented | exit 2k; the V1 increment that admits casts | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function |
1319:| OD29 | <a id="od29"></a>OWN-7: a statement expression has type `Unit` (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | only a syntactically pure non-Unit Copy value is refused (E0294); a call's result is dropped silently; bug 325 | exit 2q; exit 2j | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): a statement expression must have type `Unit`; every other unused result needs `discard(e)`; the syntactic purity heuristic is removed. Now OWN-7@3 (partial, bug 325) |
1321:| OD31 | <a id="od31"></a>ARITH-2: no `Int` fallback for an unconstrained literal (added 2026-10-29, from a Grok review of the spec) | an unconstrained integer literal is `Int` and a float literal `Float64`; bug 327 | exit 2q; exit 2j | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner ("strict across the board"): an integer literal takes its type from its context, as today; the fallback to `Int` where nothing is expected is removed, so an unconstrained literal is refused; suffixes are not required; float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Now ARITH-2@2 (partial, bug 327) |
1344:| OD54 | <a id="od54"></a>`newtype` and `.0` (added 2026-10-30, item S3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `newtype` declarations and the `.0` projection stay, or a one-field struct with a private field replaces them. Options: (a) keep `newtype` and `.0`; (b) remove both, using a one-field struct whose field is private to its module. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: no newtype; a type is made opaque by the module interface hiding its definition (a record being one possible definition) | `newtype` declarations and `.0` are accepted; `unwrap(id)` is an intrinsic | TYPE-7, TYPE-11, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
1353:| OD63 | <a id="od63"></a>Float literals take their expected type (added 2026-10-30, item Q4 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a float literal takes the float type its position expects and is refused where nothing is expected, as ARITH-2@2 states under OD31's note that float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Options: (a) yes, as ARITH-2@2 states for integers and floats alike; (b) a float literal defaults to `f64`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a). Austral: not checked | `let x = 1.5;` is accepted as `Float64` (ARITH-2@2 is partial, bug 327) | ARITH-2, ARITH-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Expected type, refused where none |
1381:| OD91 | <a id="od91"></a>A cast followed by `<` (added 2026-10-30, item LL(1) type\|5.2 of grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv). Question: How `x as T < y` parses, given that `<` after a cast's type name may start type arguments or be the less-than operator. Options: (a) a cast's type takes no type arguments unless parenthesized (`x as (T<U>)`), so `<` is less-than; (b) the parser's reading: `<` always starts type arguments, so the comparison needs parentheses (`(x as T) < y`); (c) refuse `<` directly after a cast's type and require parentheses either way. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (in the LL(1) ledger, not the picks). Austral: a conversion is the cast expression `e : T`, which takes no type arguments after it, so the conflict does not arise | the parser always reads type arguments, so `x as T < y` is refused (grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv, row type\|5.2) | SYN-1, SYN-2, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `x as T < y` is less-than; a generic cast type needs parentheses `x as (Vec<T>)` |
1387:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): every build refuses an operation without discharged evidence.** |
1388:| OD98 | <a id="od98"></a>Pointer and reference conversions written with `as` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Which conversions between raw pointers and references `as` writes (`&x as *const T`, `p as &T`, `p as &mut T`, `p as *mut U`). Bears on TRUST-1, TRUST-3, REF-5, ARITH-5. The current compiler: `&x as *const T` is accepted in safe code, `p as &T` from a raw pointer is accepted in trusted or `Unsafe` code (the binary and `--interp` agree), and a reference cast between different pointee types (`a as &Int` with `a: &A`) is refused (E0553). No entry states these: TRUST-3 forms a reference from a raw pointer with `&*p`, and REF-5 lists borrowing and projection as the ways a reference is formed. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8 kept `as` "the spelling of pointer and reference casts (`as *const T`, `as *mut T`, `as &K`, `as &mut V`)", 281 lines of std. Options: (a) `as` keeps every one of them; (b) `as` converts only a reference to a raw pointer, and a reference is formed from a raw pointer only by `&*p` (TRUST-3); (c) pointer conversions become named functions. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-98 | see OPEN-98 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reference to raw pointer conversion only in trusted/Unsafe code (equal to Austral) |
1389:| OD99 | <a id="od99"></a>The names of the named conversions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: The spellings of the named conversions of ARITH-5, ARITH-10 and ARITH-11 (`try_convert`, `truncate`, `reinterpret`, the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions, and the float roundings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`). Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10, ARITH-11. The current compiler: none of them exists (E0101). ARITH-5's sentence names `truncate` and `reinterpret`, while the archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 calls its spellings indicative, to be fixed by the API's owner; it names the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions by their rounding only. Options: (a) the spellings above, as builtins; (b) other names, or std functions rather than builtins. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-99 | see OPEN-99 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): These spellings, as builtins |
1390:| OD100 | <a id="od100"></a>Comparisons on a type parameter (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a comparison on a value of a `Copy`-bounded type parameter is accepted and checked again at each instantiation, or refused in the generic body. Bears on TYPE-6, TYPE-9, PASS-1, OPEN-42. The current compiler: `a < b` with `a, b: T` and `T: Copy` is accepted, and an instantiation at a type with no comparison is refused after monomorphization (E0229, `post-mono`); without the `Copy` bound it is refused in the body (E0229, `check`). TYPE-9's rationale says a generic body is checked once, from its bounds, which a per-instantiation check departs from. Options: (a) keep the current compiler's per-instantiation check; (b) refuse a comparison on a type parameter until a bound states it (a comparison trait); (c) refuse it. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md §8a (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-100 | see OPEN-100 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse until a bound states it |
1391:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
1392:| OD102 | <a id="od102"></a>`Float32` and `Char` as type spellings (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether `Float32` and `Char` are other spellings of `f32` and `char`, as `Float64` and `Bool` are of `f64` and `bool`. Bears on TYPE-19, SYN-3. The current compiler: the parser maps `Float64` and `Bool` to `f64` and `bool` but not `Float32` or `Char`, so `fn g(x: f32) -> Float32 { return x; }` is refused (E0220) while the resolver's `tyFromName` maps all four. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §1 names `Float32`/`f32` as two spellings. Options: (a) `Float32` and `Char` become spellings of `f32` and `char`; (b) neither, keeping the current compiler's `Float64` and `Bool`; (c) `Float32` and `Char` decided separately. Whether every capitalized spelling (`Int`, `Uint`, `Float64`, `Bool`) is removed is a separate removal question, not asked here. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-102 | see OPEN-102 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `Float32` or `Char` aliases. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: the aliases `Int`, `Uint`, `Float64` and `Bool` are removed too ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-aliases)) |
1393:| OD103 | <a id="od103"></a>`as` between reference types (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing MEMORY_SEMANTICS). Question: Whether `as` may convert a reference type to a reference type, given that REF-5 lists borrowing and projecting as the only operations that form a reference. Bears on REF-5, REF-6, ARITH-5, TRUST-4. The current compiler: an identity cast between equal reference types is accepted and denotes the operand's place (`read(r as &Int)` with `r: &Int`, and `read(&v as &Int)`, exit 42 on both consumers); every other cast between reference types, `&mut Int as &Int` included, is E0553 at core-check under ARITH-5's tag, whose sentence covers only numeric, `bool` and `char` conversions; a cast from a reference to a raw pointer is allowed (TRUST-4). The design record (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.3) listed a cast of a traced reference binding (`r as &U`) among the reference-forming operations. Options: (a) refuse `as` between reference types; (b) allow the identity cast as a no-op on the operand's place. Conversions between a reference and a raw pointer are OPEN-98's, not this question's. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the MEMORY_SEMANTICS absorption, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-103 | see OPEN-103 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
1396:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
1397:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
1404:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
1408:| OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): refusal of proven violations becomes refusal of unproven safety, everywhere.** |
1413:| OD123 | <a id="od123"></a>`as` on a newtype outside its module (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether `v as N` and `n as T`, between a newtype `N` and its inner type `T`, are accepted outside the module that declares `N`, where TYPE-11 refuses `N(v)`. Bears on TYPE-11, TYPE-24, ARITH-5, OPEN-54. The current compiler: both accepted in any module, so `x as Id` builds a foreign newtype that `Id(x)` may not (E0296), and a validated wrapper's smart constructor can be bypassed. [VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md) §8 describes the cast rule as guarding the wrapper contract from an `as` bypass, which holds for casts to unrelated types only. Options: (a) accepted anywhere, as in the current compiler; (b) accepted only where `N(v)` and `.0` are (TYPE-11, with R-0500's follow-on for `.0`); (c) `as` never names a newtype, the conversions being `N(v)` and `.0`. Austral: no newtype (OPEN-54's comparison). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-123 | see OPEN-123 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `as` never names a newtype |
1414:| OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../legacy/docs/platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
1428:| OD138 | <a id="od138"></a>Which positions give a literal its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which positions give an integer literal operand its expected type under ARITH-2@3 (and a float literal's, should OPEN-63 give floats the same rule). Bears on ARITH-2, TYPE-4 and TYPE-6. The current compiler: an unconstrained integer literal is typed `Int` and a float literal `Float64` (bug 327); with `x: u8`, a literal beside `x` takes `u8` as the other operand of `+`, `<` and `&` and as the count of `>>`: `x + 300`, `x < 300`, `x & 300` and `x >> 300` are each refused natively with E0227 (`integer literal 300 is out of range for type 'u8'`), and `300 as Int` is accepted, whether through `as` or the `Int` default the probe does not show. ARITH-2@3 refuses an integer literal with no expected type but does not list the positions that supply one. Options: (a) declared positions (an annotated binding, a parameter, a return, a field) and the other operand of a binary operator, as the probe shows the current compiler doing for `+`, `<`, `&` and a shift count (other operators were not probed); (b) declared positions only, so a literal beside a typed operand needs a typed binding; (c) option (a), plus the target type of an `as` for its literal operand. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-138 | see OPEN-138 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Declared positions + other operand of a binary op |
1435:| OD145 | <a id="od145"></a>How a contract names machine arithmetic (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: How a contract expression names machine arithmetic, which CON-3 allows in place of its default mathematical arithmetic. Bears on CON-3 and ARITH-4. The current compiler: `#[ensures(result == wrapping_add(x, 1))]` is accepted and the program runs (contracts are erased at run time); what the proof pipeline makes of the named operation was not probed. CON-3 says a contract's integer arithmetic is mathematical unless it names a machine operation, and no rule or example names one. Options: (a) the named operations of ARITH-4 (`wrapping_add`, `saturating_add` and the rest) are the machine operations in a contract; (b) a contract-only spelling; (c) contracts have only mathematical arithmetic, and CON-3 drops the exception. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-145 | see OPEN-145 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): ARITH-4 named operations |

exec
/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '186,204p' docs/proposals/STD_COVERAGE.md; sed -n '310,322p' docs/proposals/STD_COVERAGE.md; rg -n 'Q9.1|typed, versioned|assumption kinds|heap, stack|predictable policy|always abort|TYPE-4' docs/decisions.md" in /tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/qreview/codex
 succeeded in 0ms:
- **G5. Float operations beyond ARITH-1/6/11/12 (owner, S5). Stage: Wasm 26.**
  - Already covered: rounding and no contraction, comparisons, the conversions, the float-to-integer
    roundings that return `try_convert` errors, and remainder.
  - Missing, for every Wasm profile: `sqrt`; `ceil`, `floor`, `trunc` and `nearest` returning floats
    (distinct from ARITH-11's float-to-integer operations, and defined on infinities, NaNs and signed
    zeros); `min` and `max` with Wasm's NaN and signed-zero rules; `copysign`, `abs` and `neg` as
    sign-bit operations; bit reinterpretation.
  - Missing for some profiles: the saturating `trunc_sat` conversions, only if the Wasm profile
    includes them (flag F8).
  - An observation policy. Wasm lets an arithmetic NaN result have a nondeterministic sign and, in
    the relevant cases, a nondeterministic payload, while `abs`, `neg` and `copysign` are
    deterministic on bits. Reinterpretation and stores can expose those bits to later output, so
    "exclude NaNs from the comparison" is not sound.

  Wasm 21 also needs `rem_s` to return 0 for `MIN % -1`, and `div_s` there to be a Wasm trap. The
  interpreter handles both explicitly with `checked_*` (12): no gap, but a conformance item for the
  showcase.
- **G6. Large files at zero heap (showcase claim, owner). Stage: file encryptor at 18.** The sketched
  `aead::seal/open` and `poly1305::mac` are one-shot over in-memory buffers, and slice 18 has no heap.
  (evidence, or a written permission at the indirect call; 2026-11-06). Open: the callback type for
  non-consuming traversal and its stop value. Options: (a) reuse `Step` with a borrowing callback;
  (b) a coordinate cursor only (no new callback type). Recommendation: decide after D3 compares them.
- **S5. Float operations and NaN observations at 26** (G5). Options for NaNs: (a) a canonical NaN
  for arithmetic results in the Lean semantics, with the backends made to match and sign-bit
  operations exact; (b) Wasm-style allowed-result sets, with a relational comparison of observations
  that follows bits through reinterpretation and memory; (c) bit-exact backend payloads as a named
  premise. For the operations: which are primitives (`sqrt` and the float-result roundings are
  correctly rounded IEEE operations; `min`/`max` have incompatible NaN and signed-zero definitions).
  Recommendation: (a), the most transparent. The Wasm interpreter then always produces the canonical
  NaN, a member of every set Wasm allows.

